hyperactive-interaction-in-the-words-of-the-human-superorganism-lines-6 : List (List Word)
hyperactive-interaction-in-the-words-of-the-human-superorganism-lines-6 = cons "*Account* opens the oldest story about computers. *Compute* is *com* + *putare*. *Putare* first meant to prune, to cut back, to clean up, and only later to reckon and to think. *Amputate* and *dispute* keep it. To compute was once to prune together: to cut a thing down to what counts. And *count* is *computare* itself, worn down through Old French *conter*. In French, *compter* is to count and *conter* is to tell a story, and both are *computare*. *Recount* in English still does both. A computer is a counter and a teller: the machine that counts is the machine that recounts." (cons "" (cons (quoted (cons "So computing was always narration. The difference in hyperactivity is that the narration keeps its witnesses. *History* is Greek *historia*, inquiry, from *histōr*, the one who knows because they saw, the witness. *Witness*, *wit*, *wise*, *vision*, *evidence*, *video* and Greek *idea*, the form seen, all come from the root for " (cons "to see, to know" (cons ". History is witnessing kept. A proof in type theory is called a witness. Keeping every historical binding in the store is keeping witnesses. When the specializer carries a proof of its rewrite, the computation recounts itself with its witness attached. Witnessed computation is history in the oldest sense: what was seen, kept, and able to testify." nil)))) (cons "" (cons "Truth has a witness too. *True* is Old English *trēowe*, faithful, firm, from the root that also gives *tree* and *trust*. Truth was first fidelity: the firmness of a word kept. The Greek gave the other half: *alētheia*, not-forgetting. So between them the two languages say that truth is a word kept firm and not forgotten. That is a content-addressed immutable store, described in Old English and Greek." (cons "" nil)))))
